.TH DIFFVIM 1 "2026-08-10" "diffvim 1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
diffvim \- animate a code diff in vim as if a human were typing it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diffvim
.RI [ options ]
.RI <oldfile>
.RI <newfile>

.br
.B diffvim
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-multi
.RI <old1:new1>
.RI <old2:new2>
\&...

.br
.B diffvim
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-replay
.RI <file>
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR REV ]
.RB [ \-\-to
.IR REV ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diffvim
opens the old version of a file in vim and animates the transformation into
the new version character by character. The cursor glides between change
locations with smooth ease-in-out acceleration, and only the actually-changed
characters are deleted and re-typed \(em surrounding text is never touched.

After the animation completes (or the user stops it), the buffer is a normal
vim buffer and can be edited, saved, or quit normally.

Three implementations are available:
.TP
.B diffvim
Bash launcher + embedded Vimscript engine. Uses vim's native
.BR timer_start ()
for async animation. No external dependencies beyond vim 8+. Best for
everyday use.
.TP
.B diffvim-tmux
Bash orchestrator + vim in a tmux pane. Bash sends Ex commands to vim via
.BR tmux\ send-keys .
Requires bash 4+, tmux 3+, vim 8+, diff, sed, awk.
.TP
.B diffvim.pl
Perl orchestrator + vim in a tmux pane. Same architecture as diffvim-tmux
but with pluggable diff parser modules. Requires Perl 5.10+, tmux 3+, vim 8+.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-parser\  perl | diff2html
(diffvim.pl only) Diff parser to use.
.B perl
uses a pure-Perl LCS algorithm (default, no deps).
.B diff2html
shells out to the
.B diff2html
CLI with
.B \-f json
for line-level parsing.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed\  N
Speed multiplier. 0.5 = half speed, 2 = double speed, 5 = 5x speed. All
timing delays are divided by this value. Can also be set via the
.B DIFFVIM_SPEED
environment variable.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\  FILE
After the animation completes, write the buffer to
.I FILE
and quit vim. Useful for scripted use where you want the result saved
without manual intervention.
.TP
.BI \-\-context\  N
Fold unchanged regions longer than 2*N lines, keeping N lines of context
around each hunk. This makes large-file diffs easier to follow by hiding
unchanged regions. Default: 0 (no folding).
.TP
.BI \-\-max-hunk-chars\  N
If a hunk has more than N changed characters, skip the char-by-char
animation and apply the entire hunk instantly. Useful for large changes
that would take too long to animate character by character. Default: 0
(no limit).
.TP
.BI \-\-max-word-chars\  N
If a contiguous sequence of changed characters forms a word (non-space
characters terminated by a space or newline) and the word length is <= N,
type the entire word instantly, then pause. This makes the animation
feel more natural \(em short words are typed as units rather than
character by character. Default: 0 (disabled).
.TP
.BI \-\-word-pause-ms\  N
Pause in milliseconds after an instant word is applied (only relevant
with
.BR \-\-max-word-chars ).
Default: 150. Can also be set via
.BR DIFFVIM_WORD_PAUSE_MS .
.TP
.BR \-\-scroll\  zz | zt | zb | none
Scroll the cursor to the specified position during animation:
.RS
.TP
.B zz
Center the cursor on screen
.TP
.B zt
Position the cursor at the top of the screen
.TP
.B zb
Position the cursor at the bottom of the screen
.TP
.B none
Don't force scroll (default)
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-multi
Treat arguments as
.I old:new
pairs for multi-file animation. After finishing one file, the animation
transitions to the next with a brief "next file" message.
.TP
.B \-\-replay
Animate git history for the given file(s). Requires
.B git
in PATH. For each file, extracts the old version at each commit in the
range and animates the transformation to the next commit, ending with
the working copy.
.TP
.BI \-\-from\  REV
(diffvim-tmux and diffvim.pl only) Git revision to start replay from.
Default: HEAD~5. Only used with
.BR \-\-replay .
.TP
.BI \-\-to\  REV
(diffvim-tmux and diffvim.pl only) Git revision to end replay at.
Default: HEAD. Only used with
.BR \-\-replay .
.TP
.BR \-h ,\  \-\-help
Show help message and exit.

.SH CONTROLS
During animation, the following keys are active in vim normal mode:
.TP
.B <Space>
Pause / resume the animation. While paused, a "PAUSED" indicator is shown.
.TP
.B n
Skip the current hunk: apply all remaining character operations instantly
and move to the next hunk.
.TP
.B b
Back to the previous hunk: revert the buffer to the state before the
current hunk started and replay it from the beginning.
.TP
.B q
Stop the animation. The buffer is left in its current state for normal
editing. If
.B \-\-output
was specified, the buffer is written to the output file before stopping.
.TP
.B +
Speed up: multiply the current speed by 1.5. The new speed is shown in
the status message.
.TP
.B \-
Slow down: divide the current speed by 1.5.
.TP
.B =
Reset speed to 1.0x.
.TP
.B ?
(diffvim only) Show help: current hunk index and keymap reminder.

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
All timing values can be set via environment variables. Command-line options
override environment variables, which override built-in defaults.
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_TICK_MS
Animation frame interval in milliseconds (default: 16, ~60fps).
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_TYPE_DELAY_MS
Delay between typed characters in milliseconds (default: 35).
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_DELETE_DELAY_MS
Delay between deleted characters in milliseconds (default: 25).
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_MOVE_MIN_MS
Minimum cursor glide duration in milliseconds (default: 200).
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_MOVE_MAX_MS
Maximum cursor glide duration in milliseconds (default: 1400).
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_MOVE_MS_PER_UNIT
Milliseconds per unit of glide distance (default: 6). Lines are weighted
80x more than columns.
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_HUNK_PAUSE_MS
Pause between hunks in milliseconds (default: 180).
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_WORD_PAUSE_MS
Pause after instant word in milliseconds (default: 150). Only relevant
with
.BR \-\-max-word-chars .
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_SPEED
Speed multiplier (default: 1.0). Same as
.BR \-\-speed .

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Basic usage:
.B diffvim old.py new.py
.TP
Slow down for a presentation:
.B diffvim \-\-speed 0.5 old.py new.py
.TP
Speed up for a quick review:
.B diffvim \-\-speed 3 old.py new.py
.TP
Write result to a file and quit:
.B diffvim \-\-output result.py old.py new.py
.TP
Center cursor during animation:
.B diffvim \-\-scroll zz old.py new.py
.TP
Skip large hunks, type short words instantly:
.B diffvim \-\-max-hunk-chars 200 \-\-max-word-chars 5 old.py new.py
.TP
Multi-file animation:
.B diffvim \-\-multi old1.py:new1.py old2.py:new2.py
.TP
Replay git history (last 5 commits):
.B diffvim \-\-replay src/main.py
.TP
Replay a specific commit range:
.B diffvim \-\-replay src/main.py \-\-from v1.0 \-\-to HEAD
.TP
Animate a git diff:
.B git\ show\ HEAD:file.py\ >\ /tmp/old.py && diffvim /tmp/old.py file.py

.SH ARCHITECTURE
The animation uses a two-level diff:
.IP 1. 3
Line-level LCS diff identifies changed regions (hunks).
.IP 2. 3
Within each hunk, char-level LCS diff produces minimal character
operations (keep / delete / insert).
.PP
The cursor glides between change locations using an ease-in-out cubic
curve. Glide duration is proportional to distance (lines weighted 80x
more than columns), clamped to [MOVE_MIN_MS, MOVE_MAX_MS].

In the
.B diffvim
implementation, vim's
.BR timer_start ()
drives the animation asynchronously. In
.B diffvim-tmux
and
.BR diffvim.pl ,
the orchestrator sends Ex commands to vim via
.B tmux\ send-keys
and sleeps between steps.

User input is handled via vim normal-mode mappings. In
.BR diffvim ,
mappings call vimscript functions directly. In the tmux-based
implementations, mappings write single-character commands to a named
pipe (FIFO) that the orchestrator reads non-blocking.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I DiffVim/Parser/Perl.pm
Pure-Perl LCS diff parser module (used by diffvim.pl).
.TP
.I DiffVim/Parser/Diff2Html.pm
diff2html CLI-based parser module (used by diffvim.pl).
.TP
.I plugin/diffvim.vim
Vim plugin providing the
.B :Diffvim
command for running inside an existing vim session.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vim (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR diff (1),
.BR git (1)

.SH BUGS
.TP
Trailing newline changes
If the only difference between old and new is a trailing newline, the
animation may not handle it correctly.
.TP
tmux send-keys race conditions
.B diffvim-tmux
and
.B diffvim.pl
can experience race conditions where Ex command text leaks into normal
mode when commands are sent faster than vim processes them. The
.B diffvim
implementation does not have this issue.
.TP
Large files
The LCS dynamic programming table is O(N\(muM) in memory. For files with
more than 10,000 lines, memory usage may be significant.

